---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

/**
 * DRAFT (marketing research 2026-11-04, branch site/program-pages). Not on main.
 *
 * Program landing page for the 5/3/1 Boring But Big routine. Every program
 * fact was read from public/programs/531-bbb.badger (exported by the app,
 * app_version 1.1.15 on 2026-10-23). Re-check against that file whenever the
 * gallery is regenerated.
 *
 * Trademark hygiene as on the 5x5 page: compatibility, never affiliation.
 */

const description =
  'Run 5/3/1 Boring But Big in Badger: a training max per lift, every set as a percentage of it, the four-week wave, and a training max that rises after each cycle. What it does and what you decide.';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is there a 5/3/1 app that does the percentages for me?',
    a: 'Yes. Badger keeps a training max per lift, writes every working set as a percentage of it, and rounds each load to plates your gym has. You set the four training maxes once before the first workout.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Badger raise my training max after a cycle?',
    a: 'Yes. After the four-week cycle it adds 2.5 kg to press and bench and 5 kg to squat and deadlift, unless a plus set fell short of the minimum. In that case the training max holds and the preview says so.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it include the Boring But Big sets?',
    a: 'Yes. After the main lift each day, the routine has five sets of ten of the same lift at 60% of the training max, plus accessories.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it work in pounds?',
    a: 'Yes. Weights can be logged in kilograms or pounds, and per exercise if you want.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does Badger cost?',
    a: 'Nothing. There is no account and no cap on routines or history.',
  },
];
---

<HelpLayout title="5/3/1 app and tracker" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="5/3/1 Boring But Big in Badger"
    description={description}
    dateModified="2026-11-04"
    about={['5/3/1', 'Boring But Big']}
    breadcrumb={[{ name: 'Guides', url: '/guides/' }]}
  />
  <script
    type="application/ld+json"
    slot="head"
    set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: f.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
      })),
    })}
  />

  <h1>5/3/1 Boring But Big in Badger</h1>

  <p>
    Badger runs the 5/3/1 wave from a training max and moves that number up after each cycle. You
    set the four maxes once. This page covers what the routine contains, what you still decide, and
    how to bring your history across.
  </p>

  <h2 id="whats-in-the-box">What the routine contains</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Four days, one main lift each.</strong> Press day, deadlift day, bench day and squat
      day, set for Monday, Wednesday, Thursday and Saturday. You can move them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The four-week wave.</strong> Week 1 is 65, 75 and 85% for fives. Week 2 is 70, 80 and
      90% for threes. Week 3 is 75, 85 and 95% for 5, 3 and 1. Week 4 is the light week at 40, 50
      and 60%. The top set of weeks 1 to 3 is a plus set: take it as far as you can.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Boring But Big.</strong> After the main lift, five sets of ten of the same lift at 60%
      of the training max, then accessories such as lateral raises, curls, leg curls and rows.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Loads your plates can make.</strong> Each percentage is turned into a weight using the
      bar and plates you have set up, and the plate calculator shows what goes on each side.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The training max moves itself.</strong> After the four weeks it adds 2.5 kg to press
      and bench and 5 kg to squat and deadlift. If a plus set missed the minimum, the max holds for
      that cycle and the preview says why.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-you-decide">What you still decide</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The training maxes.</strong> Set one for each of the four lifts before your first
      workout. The usual advice is 85 to 90% of a recent best, and the number is yours to pick. See
      <a href="/help/progression/#training-max">training max</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Which week you are on.</strong> Starting mid-cycle or coming back from a break? Set the
      week by hand.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every weight.</strong> The proposal is a starting point. Type over it and the rule
      keeps running.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="caps">The short version</span>
    <p>
      Set four training maxes, start week 1, and Badger shows the weight for every set, then raises
      the maxes when the cycle ends.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="get-it">Get the routine</h2>

  <p>
    In the app, go to <strong>You &rarr; Routines</strong> and open the Intermediate Programs folder.
    Or download <code>531-bbb.badger</code> from the <a href="/programs/">programs page</a> and open
    it on your phone. nSuns 531LP ships the same way if you want a higher-volume version.
  </p>

  <h2 id="import">Bringing your history over</h2>

  <p>
    Export a CSV from Strong, Hevy, FitNotes or StrongLifts, then use <strong>Settings &rarr; Backup,
    import and export &rarr; Import from another app</strong>. The details are in
    <a href="/guides/switching/">switching from another app</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="questions">Questions</h2>

  {faq.map((f) => (
    <>
      <h3>{f.q}</h3>
      <p>{f.a}</p>
    </>
  ))}

  <h2 id="trademark">About the name</h2>

  <p class="fineprint">
    5/3/1 is the work of Jim Wendler. Badger is not affiliated with, endorsed by or connected to him
    or to his publisher. This page describes a routine written for Badger from the publicly known
    shape of the program.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
